      { item: "3.1.1", q: "Which statement best describes the particles in a liquid?", options: ["Close together but able to move past one another", "Far apart, moving randomly at high speed with negligible forces between them", "Arranged in a regular lattice with no motion", "Fixed in place, vibrating about equilibrium positions"], answer: 0, explain: "Liquid particles are about as close as in a solid, but bonds are constantly broken and re-formed, so they can flow past each other." },
      { item: "3.1.1", q: "Why are gases much easier to compress than solids or liquids?", options: ["Gas particles are smaller", "Gas particles move more slowly", "Gas particles are far apart compared with their size", "Gas particles have no mass"], answer: 2, explain: "The mean separation in a gas is about 10 times the particle diameter, so most of a gas's volume is empty space." },
      { item: "3.1.1", q: "When a solid melts, the mean separation of its particles typically…", options: ["stays exactly the same", "increases by a factor of about 10", "increases only slightly", "decreases to zero"], answer: 2, explain: "Solids and liquids have similar densities. The big jump in separation (×10) happens on boiling, not melting." },
      { item: "3.1.2", q: "A block measures 0.20 m × 0.10 m × 0.050 m and has a mass of 2.7 kg. What is its density?", options: ["270 kg m⁻³", "1350 kg m⁻³", "2700 kg m⁻³", "27 000 kg m⁻³"], answer: 2, explain: "V = 0.20 × 0.10 × 0.050 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³. ρ = m/V = 2.7/1.0 × 10⁻³ = 2700 kg m⁻³." },
      { item: "3.1.2", q: "Water boils to form steam. The mass stays the same. What happens to the density?", options: ["It stays the same, because the mass is unchanged", "It decreases slightly", "It increases a lot", "It decreases a lot, because the volume increases greatly"], answer: 3, explain: "ρ = m/V. Steam occupies about 1000 times the volume of the same mass of water, so its density is much lower." },
      { item: "3.1.3", q: "What is 27 °C in kelvin?", options: ["−246 K", "27 K", "300 K", "373 K"], answer: 2, explain: "T(K) = θ(°C) + 273, so 27 + 273 = 300 K." },
      { item: "3.1.3", q: "The temperature of a liquid rises by 15 °C. What is the rise in kelvin?", options: ["15 K", "288 K", "258 K", "It depends on the starting temperature"], answer: 0, explain: "One kelvin is the same size as one degree Celsius, so temperature differences are identical on both scales." },
      { item: "3.1.3", q: "What is special about 0 K (absolute zero)?", options: ["It is the freezing point of water", "It is the temperature at which particles have the minimum possible kinetic energy", "It is the lowest temperature reached in a laboratory freezer", "It is equal to −100 °C"], answer: 1, explain: "Absolute zero (−273 °C) is the lowest possible temperature, where the random kinetic energy of particles is a minimum." },
      { item: "3.1.4", q: "The Kelvin temperature of a gas is doubled. What happens to the average kinetic energy of its particles?", options: ["It halves", "It stays the same", "It doubles", "It increases by a factor of 4"], answer: 2, explain: "Average Eₖ = (3/2)k_B T, so average kinetic energy is directly proportional to Kelvin temperature." },
      { item: "3.1.4", q: "A gas is heated from 27 °C to 54 °C. By what factor does the average kinetic energy of its particles increase?", options: ["4.0", "0.5", "2.0", "1.09"], answer: 3, explain: "Use kelvin: 300 K → 327 K. 327/300 = 1.09. Doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the energy." },
      { item: "3.1.4", q: "What is the average translational kinetic energy of a gas particle at 300 K? (k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹)", options: ["6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J", "4.1 × 10⁻²³ J", "1.2 × 10⁻²⁰ J", "4.1 × 10⁻²¹ J"], answer: 0, explain: "Eₖ = (3/2)k_B T = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = 6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J." },
      { item: "3.1.5", q: "Internal energy is defined as…", options: ["the temperature of the object multiplied by its mass", "the total kinetic energy of the object as it moves", "the total random kinetic energy plus the total intermolecular potential energy of the particles", "the thermal energy transferred to the object"], answer: 2, explain: "Internal energy is the sum of the random kinetic energies and the intermolecular potential energies of all the particles." },
      { item: "3.1.5", q: "A block of ice at 0 °C melts to water at 0 °C. What happens to its internal energy?", options: ["It increases, because the intermolecular potential energy increases", "It stays the same, because the temperature is constant", "It decreases, because the bonds break", "It increases, because the particles move faster"], answer: 0, explain: "Energy is absorbed during melting. Temperature (average Eₖ) is constant, so the extra energy goes into intermolecular potential energy." },
      { item: "3.1.5", q: "Why is the internal energy of an ideal gas only kinetic?", options: ["There are no intermolecular forces, so there is no intermolecular potential energy", "Ideal gas particles never collide", "Ideal gases are always at high temperature", "Ideal gas particles have no mass"], answer: 0, explain: "The ideal gas model assumes no forces between particles except in collisions, so potential energy is zero." },
      { item: "3.1.6", q: "A small cup of water at 60 °C is placed in a large bath at 30 °C. The bath has more internal energy. In which direction is the net thermal energy transfer?", options: ["Equal transfer in both directions", "From the bath to the cup, because the bath has more internal energy", "From the cup to the bath, because the cup is at a higher temperature", "No transfer, because they have different masses"], answer: 2, explain: "Net thermal energy always flows from higher to lower temperature, regardless of which object has more internal energy." },
      { item: "3.1.6", q: "Two objects are in thermal equilibrium. Which statement must be true?", options: ["They have the same specific heat capacity", "No energy is transferred between their particles at all", "They have the same internal energy", "They have the same temperature"], answer: 3, explain: "Thermal equilibrium means equal temperature, so there is no net transfer. Particles still exchange energy, but equally in both directions." },
      { item: "3.1.7", q: "On a heating curve, why is the line horizontal while a substance boils?", options: ["The heater is switched off", "Energy supplied increases intermolecular potential energy, not average kinetic energy", "The particles lose kinetic energy as fast as they gain it", "The specific heat capacity becomes zero"], answer: 1, explain: "During a phase change the energy separates the particles (raises potential energy). Average Eₖ, and so temperature, stays constant." },
      { item: "3.1.7", q: "Why does boiling 1 kg of water need much more energy than melting 1 kg of ice?", options: ["Boiling happens at a higher temperature", "Steam particles have more kinetic energy", "Water has a higher specific heat capacity than ice", "Boiling separates the molecules completely, so much more work is done against intermolecular forces"], answer: 3, explain: "Melting only loosens the structure. Boiling moves the molecules far apart, which needs far more energy, so L_v > L_f." },
      { item: "3.1.7", q: "Steam at 100 °C condenses to water at 100 °C. Which is correct?", options: ["Energy is released and the temperature stays constant", "Energy is released and the temperature falls", "No energy transfer takes place", "Energy is absorbed and the temperature rises"], answer: 0, explain: "Condensing releases the latent heat as intermolecular potential energy decreases. The temperature does not change during the phase change." },
      { item: "3.1.8", q: "How much energy is needed to heat 0.50 kg of water from 20 °C to 40 °C? (c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)", options: ["4.2 × 10³ J", "8.4 × 10⁴ J", "4.2 × 10⁴ J", "2.1 × 10⁴ J"], answer: 2, explain: "Q = mcΔT = 0.50 × 4200 × 20 = 4.2 × 10⁴ J." },
      { item: "3.1.8", q: "How much energy is needed to melt 0.20 kg of ice at 0 °C? (L_f = 3.3 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹)", options: ["6.6 × 10⁴ J", "1.7 × 10⁶ J", "3.3 × 10⁴ J", "6.6 × 10⁵ J"], answer: 0, explain: "Q = mL = 0.20 × 3.3 × 10⁵ = 6.6 × 10⁴ J. No ΔT term, because temperature is constant." },
      { item: "3.1.8", q: "A 50 W heater supplies energy to a 0.40 kg metal block for 120 s, and its temperature rises by 15 K. Assuming no losses, what is the specific heat capacity?", options: ["250 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹", "400 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹", "1000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹", "6000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "Q = Pt = 50 × 120 = 6000 J. c = Q/(mΔT) = 6000/(0.40 × 15) = 1000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹." }
