      { item: "3.2.3", q: "Which of these is an assumption of the ideal gas model?", options: ["Molecules attract each other weakly", "Collisions between molecules lose kinetic energy", "The volume of the molecules is negligible compared with the volume of the container", "All molecules move at the same speed"], answer: 2, explain: "The model assumes point-like molecules, no forces except in collisions, elastic collisions and random motion with a range of speeds." },
      { item: "3.2.3", q: "Why do real gases deviate from ideal behaviour at very high pressure?", options: ["The molecules move faster", "The molecules' own volume and intermolecular forces are no longer negligible", "Collisions become perfectly elastic", "The number of moles changes"], answer: 1, explain: "When molecules are squeezed close together, their size and attractive forces matter, so the ideal assumptions fail." },
      { item: "3.2.4", q: "A fixed mass of gas at constant temperature is compressed from 6.0 L to 2.0 L. The initial pressure is 100 kPa. What is the final pressure?", options: ["33 kPa", "200 kPa", "300 kPa", "600 kPa"], answer: 2, explain: "Boyle's law: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂, so p₂ = 100 × 6.0/2.0 = 300 kPa." },
      { item: "3.2.4", q: "A fixed mass of gas at constant pressure is heated from 300 K to 450 K. Its volume was 2.0 L. What is the new volume?", options: ["4.5 L", "2.0 L", "1.3 L", "3.0 L"], answer: 3, explain: "Charles's law: V/T is constant, so V₂ = 2.0 × 450/300 = 3.0 L." },
      { item: "3.2.4", q: "For a fixed mass of gas at constant volume, which graph is a straight line through the origin?", options: ["Pressure against Kelvin temperature", "Pressure against 1/Kelvin temperature", "Volume against Kelvin temperature", "Pressure against Celsius temperature"], answer: 0, explain: "p ∝ T only when T is in kelvin. Against Celsius, the line meets the temperature axis at −273 °C." },
      { item: "3.2.5", q: "2.0 mol of an ideal gas is at 300 K in a volume of 0.050 m³. What is its pressure? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)", options: ["5.0 × 10³ Pa", "1.0 × 10⁴ Pa", "1.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "2.5 × 10⁵ Pa"], answer: 2, explain: "p = nRT/V = 2.0 × 8.31 × 300/0.050 = 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa." },
      { item: "3.2.5", q: "Which equation links pressure, volume and temperature to the NUMBER OF MOLECULES N?", options: ["pV = Nk_BT", "pV = NRT", "p = (1/3)ρv²", "pV = nRT"], answer: 0, explain: "pV = Nk_BT uses molecules and the Boltzmann constant; pV = nRT uses moles. R = N_A k_B links them." },
      { item: "3.2.5", q: "A sealed rigid container of gas is heated from 27 °C to 327 °C. By what factor does the pressure change?", options: ["×1.1", "×12", "×2", "×0.5"], answer: 2, explain: "p/T is constant at constant V and n. In kelvin: 600/300 = 2. Always convert to kelvin first." },
      { item: "3.2.6", q: "A molecule of mass m moving at speed v hits a wall at right angles and rebounds elastically. What is the magnitude of its change in momentum?", options: ["0", "mv", "2mv", "½mv²"], answer: 2, explain: "Momentum changes from +mv to −mv, so |Δp| = 2mv." },
      { item: "3.2.6", q: "A gas has density 1.2 kg m⁻³ and pressure 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. What is the root mean square speed of its molecules?", options: ["250 000 m s⁻¹", "910 m s⁻¹", "290 m s⁻¹", "500 m s⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "p = (1/3)ρv², so v² = 3p/ρ = 3 × 1.0 × 10⁵/1.2 = 2.5 × 10⁵, giving v = 500 m s⁻¹." },
      { item: "3.2.6", q: "The Kelvin temperature of a gas is multiplied by 4. What happens to the rms speed of its molecules?", options: ["It doubles", "It is multiplied by 4", "It is multiplied by 16", "It is unchanged"], answer: 0, explain: "Average Eₖ ∝ T, so v² ∝ T and v ∝ √T. √4 = 2." },
      { item: "3.2.7", q: "What is the internal energy of 1.0 mol of an ideal monatomic gas at 300 K? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)", options: ["2.5 × 10³ J", "3.7 × 10³ J", "7.5 × 10³ J", "1.2 × 10³ J"], answer: 1, explain: "U = (3/2)nRT = 1.5 × 1.0 × 8.31 × 300 = 3.7 × 10³ J." },
      { item: "3.2.7", q: "Container A holds twice as many atoms of an ideal monatomic gas as container B, at the same temperature. How do their internal energies compare?", options: ["U_A = 4U_B", "U_A = ½U_B", "U_A = U_B", "U_A = 2U_B"], answer: 3, explain: "U = (3/2)Nk_BT. At the same T, U is proportional to the number of atoms." },
      { item: "3.2.8", q: "Under which conditions does a real gas behave most like an ideal gas?", options: ["Low pressure and high temperature", "High pressure and high temperature", "Low pressure and low temperature", "High pressure and low temperature"], answer: 0, explain: "Low pressure (low density) makes molecular volume negligible; high temperature makes intermolecular forces negligible compared with Eₖ." },
      { item: "3.2.8", q: "Why does a real gas behave less ideally at low temperature?", options: ["The pressure always rises", "The molecules get larger", "The molecules move slowly, so intermolecular attractions become significant", "The collisions become more elastic"], answer: 2, explain: "At low Eₖ, attractive forces noticeably affect the motion, and the gas gets close to liquefying." }
